# Spec 019 — Matches Shortlist

Status: Draft
Owner: <you>
Related: 001 R4 (budget de-emphasis), 009 R3/R6 (commute-reach check +
top movers — the list-that-flies pattern this reuses), 014 (price-to-income
metric — the ranking signal), 016 (dual workplaces — matches respect the
intersection). First thin slice of the long-deferred "optimization" roadmap
item (CLAUDE.md).

## Overview

The map overlays affordability and commute but never states the answer: a
user must visually hunt for ZIPs that are simultaneously un-hatched and
inside the drive-time bands. This spec adds a ranked, clickable "Your
matches" list — the ZIPs that are both within budget and within commute
reach — computed entirely client-side from data the app has already
loaded. No new endpoints, no additional Mapbox spend.

## Requirements

- **R1 — Match computation.** A pure function derives the shortlist from
  the loaded records, ZIP centroids, the active isochrone collection, and
  the budget. A ZIP matches when its centroid lies inside the "typical"
  (midday) reach band — the app's established honest default; if the
  collection carries no typical band (an upstream scenario was skipped),
  containment in any band qualifies. With a budget set, `median_value`
  must also be ≤ budget; with no budget, reach alone qualifies. In dual
  mode the input collection is already the client-side intersection
  (016), so matches are ZIPs both workplaces can reach — no extra logic.
- **R2 — Ranked panel.** A "Your matches" fold in the controls panel
  (above Top movers) lists the top 8 matches with place name, median
  value, and the price-to-income multiple; the summary line shows the
  total match count. Ranking: price-to-income ascending (the app's own
  affordability signal), ZIPs without the ratio after those with it,
  median value ascending as the tiebreak. Clicking a row selects and
  flies to the ZIP (same behavior as Top movers).
- **R3 — Empty and absent states.** No isochrone yet (still loading, or
  fetch failed) → the panel does not render at all. Reach present but
  zero matches → the fold renders a one-line nudge ("No ZIPs are within
  both budget and reach — try raising either"). The list re-derives
  live when budget, commute time, mode, pins, or state change.

## Non-functional / Constraints

- No new dependencies and no backend changes: reuses `centroidsByZip`,
  `scenariosContaining`, and the records map already in memory.
- The computation is memoized and O(ZIPs × bands); it must not
  perceptibly lag pin drags in the largest states (TX ≈ 2k ZIPs).
- Panel follows the existing fold pattern (`details.panel-fold`) and the
  40/44 px touch-target rules (010 R5).

## Acceptance Criteria

- [ ] WA, budget $600k, 30-min drive from the default pin → the fold
      lists ≤ 8 ZIPs, each inside the midday band and ≤ $600k; the
      summary shows the true total; clicking a row opens that ZIP's
      detail panel and flies to it.
- [ ] Clearing the budget widens the list (reach-only); shortening the
      commute to 15 min shrinks it; switching to walk mode re-derives it.
- [ ] Add a second workplace far away → matches come only from the
      intersection; an empty intersection yields the empty-state nudge.
- [ ] Before the first isochrone resolves (and when it fails), no
      matches fold is present.
- [ ] `npm run test` green, including new unit tests for the match/rank
      logic and component tests for the panel states.

## Out of Scope (explicit)

- Server-side or multi-metric scoring (blended cost surface remains a
  future spec); user-tunable ranking weights.
- Monthly-payment affordability math (candidate spec 020).
- Persisting or sharing the shortlist beyond the existing URL state.

## Verification notes

R1 → matches unit tests (containment, budget edge, typical-band
fallback, ranking, tiebreaks); R2/R3 → matchesPanel component tests +
manual walk of the acceptance list against a running app. The human
verifies; the implementing agent does not self-certify done.
